Ladybird now sizes an auto-width table against the space its wrapper
has, never below its minimum inline size, and makes the wrapper as wide
as the table box, so the captions take the table’s inline size, and a
table too wide for the space next to floats goes below them. Blink lays
captions out at the table’s inline size in its LayoutCaption() function,
and BlockLayoutAlgorithm::LayoutNewFormattingContext() lays a table out
in each layout opportunity next to the floats, moving on to the next
one while the table’s fragment is wider than the opportunity. WebKit’s
RenderTable::layoutCaption() lays captions out in the table’s logical
width, and RenderBlockFlow::computedClearDeltaForChild() moves a box
that avoids floats down until its border box fits next to them. Gecko’s
nsTableWrapperFrame::Reflow() sizes the inner table first and then the
caption, and BlockReflowState::FloatAvoidingBlockFitsInAvailSpace()
decides if a float-avoiding block fits next to the floats. Chrome
158, Firefox 151 and WebKit 26.5 agree with Ladybird now on all
22 cases of the two tests.
